--- Analysis of the 3 remaining lifecycle-matrix.test.ts failures (not fixed; out of my file boundary and not production defects) ---
1) "a lost claim response, a bridge crash, and a server restart converge to exactly one active
   Claim" (line 610): fails with claim_id_required on the post-restart heartbeat. Root cause:
   the test spawns `first`/`second` bridges with `origin: proxyOrigin` (the response-loss test
   double) for the claim, but then spawns `third` with `origin: restarted.origin` (the REAL
   server's own origin, a different literal URL/port than the proxy) for the report_progress
   call. apps/mcp/src/main.ts's receipt addressing (originDigest/receiptFilePath) is a pure,
   deterministic hash of the literal --url origin by design -- an explicit, heavily-pinned
   Issue #32 contract (see claim-receipt.test.ts's "different tasks and different server
   origins coexist without interference", which asserts two different origins must NOT share a
   receipt). The third bridge process therefore legitimately looks up a receipt file under a
   different origin digest than the one `first`/`second` wrote under, finds none, and correctly
   omits claim_id -- which the real server correctly refuses with claim_id_required. The proxy
   was still running at that point (never closed until test teardown) and forwards to the same
   backend, so pointing `third` at `proxyOrigin` (matching `first`/`second`) instead of
   `restarted.origin` would very likely make this scenario pass. This looks like a test
   authoring inconsistency, not a bridge defect; I have not edited the test file.

2) "a lost submit_pr response and a bridge crash converge to exactly one terminal Claim..."
   (line 669): fails at line 723 on `submissionsAfterRetry[0].prState` (camelCase) being
   `undefined`. `submissionRowsForTask` runs a raw better-sqlite3 `SELECT submissions.*` query,
   which returns actual SQL column names (snake_case: `pr_state`, per schema.ts:126
   `prState: text('pr_state')`), never the Drizzle TS property name `prState`. Lines 885 and
   1046 of this exact same test file correctly read `.pr_state` off the same helper's rows; line
   723 is the one outlier using `.prState`. A plain naming-mismatch bug in the test, unrelated
   to apps/mcp/src/main.ts.

3) "claim_task/report_progress/submit_pr under KAOLA_CARRIER=runner reach the same
   Task/Claim/submission outcome..." (line 845): fails because `assert.deepEqual(runnerOutcome,
   directOutcome, ...)` includes `submissionPrUrl` in the comparison, but the two fixtures use
   genuinely different repo full names (`kaola/carrier-direct` vs `kaola/carrier-runner`), so
   their real PR URLs (which embed the full name) can never be equal by construction. The
   deepEqual appears to need to exclude the fixture-specific `submissionPrUrl` field (or compare
   only its *shape*, not its exact value) from the carrier-parity check. Unrelated to
   apps/mcp/src/main.ts.

All three are reported here per the "STOP and report, do not edit a test I don't hold custody
of" protocol; apps/server/ was also explicitly off-limits for this task.
